    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most people who start running on a treadmill already know how to use one. They walk on it,
          they know where the stop button is, and they want to turn the walk into a run without
          getting hurt or giving up in week three. That transition is mostly a question of
          patience rather than fitness: run in short pieces, run slower than feels necessary, run
          on alternate days, and let your legs catch up with your lungs. This page covers what to
          do in the first sessions, the speeds and incline to set, the treadmill-specific habits
          that keep you safe on a moving belt, a simple way to progress over four to eight weeks,
          and the signs that mean ease off or see someone.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. How to Start Running on a Treadmill: The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Start with walk-run intervals — about one minute of easy jogging followed by
            two minutes of walking, repeated six to eight times — at a pace slow enough to talk in
            short sentences, three times a week on non-consecutive days.</strong> Add running time
            only when the current session feels manageable and nothing hurts the next day, and
            expect it to take somewhere between four and eight weeks of consistent sessions to reach
            about 20 minutes of continuous easy running, and a few weeks more for 30.
          </p>
          <p>
            For most beginners, "easy jogging" on a treadmill lands somewhere around 4 to 5.5 mph
            (a 15- to 11-minute mile), with walking breaks at roughly 3 to 3.5 mph. Those are typical
            ranges, not targets: the right number is whatever lets you pass the talk test. Set the
            incline to 0 or 1% and leave it there. Clip the safety key on every session, warm up
            with five minutes of walking, cool down with five more, and keep your hands off the
            rails once you are moving.
          </p>
          <p>
            The rest of this page explains each of those choices. The two ideas that matter most
            are that running time is built before speed, and that your heart and lungs adapt faster
            than your bones and tendons. Feeling able to do more is not, on its own, a reason to do
            more.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="doctor-first" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Should You See a Doctor Before You Start Running?</h2>
<p>
            Most healthy adults who already walk regularly can start a gentle walk-run program
            without a medical visit. Some people should check first, and the screening approach
            used in exercise physiology is a useful guide to who they are.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Talk to a doctor before you start if any of these apply:</strong>
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>You have a known heart, lung, kidney or metabolic condition, such as heart disease,
            COPD or diabetes.</li>
            <li>You have ever had chest pain or pressure, fainting, unexplained breathlessness or
            palpitations during exertion, or you get them now during brisk walking.</li>
            <li>You have high blood pressure, or take medication that affects your heart rate or
            blood pressure.</li>
            <li>You are pregnant or recently postpartum.</li>
            <li>You have a joint, back or foot problem, a recent injury or recent surgery.</li>
            <li>You have been largely inactive for a long time and are older or carrying a lot of
            extra weight.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            None of these necessarily rules running out. They mean the right starting point,
            intensity and rate of progress are a conversation with someone who knows your history.
            A physical therapist is the right person for joint and injury questions; a doctor for
            anything involving the heart, lungs, blood pressure or medication.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>During any session, stop and get checked</strong> if you feel chest pain or
            tightness, breathlessness out of proportion to the effort, dizziness or light-headedness,
            an irregular or racing heartbeat, or sudden sharp pain in a joint. Slow the belt to a
            walk, then stop it, and step off once it has stopped — do not jump off a moving belt.
            Our guide to whether
            <a href="/is-running-on-a-treadmill-bad-for-you/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">running on a treadmill is bad for you</a>
            covers the real risks and the myths in more detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="first-sessions" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. What Should Your First Running Sessions Look Like?</h2>
<p>
            Short pieces of slow running separated by walking. The walking breaks are not a
            concession; they are what lets a beginner accumulate several minutes of running without
            the fatigue that makes form collapse and joints complain.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">A first session, start to finish</h3>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Five minutes of walking</strong>, starting easy and building to a brisk walk.
            This is the warm-up, and our
            <a href="/treadmill-warm-up-and-cool-down/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">warm-up and cool-down guide</a>
            explains why it matters more for running than walking.</li>
            <li><strong>One minute of easy jogging.</strong> Raise the speed in small steps from your
            brisk walk to a pace just fast enough that you are running rather than walking — both
            feet briefly off the belt — and no faster.</li>
            <li><strong>Two minutes of walking</strong> at your brisk walking speed.</li>
            <li><strong>Repeat the jog and walk</strong> until you have done six rounds. If six feels
            easy, do eight. If four is plenty, stop at four.</li>
            <li><strong>Five minutes of easy walking</strong> to cool down, slowing over the last
            couple of minutes.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            That is roughly 28 to 34 minutes on the belt, of which six to eight are running. It
            should feel too easy to many people. That is deliberate: the aim of the first week is
            three completed sessions and nothing sore, not a hard workout.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Conversational pace, and the talk test</h3>
          <p>
            The single most useful rule for a new runner is to run at a pace where you could speak
            in short sentences. If you can only manage a word or two, you are going too fast,
            whatever the display says. If you could sing, you could go a little quicker. This
            "conversational pace" keeps you in the low-intensity range where aerobic fitness is
            built and recovery is quick, and it is the pace most of your running should stay at
            for months, not just weeks.
          </p>
          <p>
            Your heart rate will climb quickly during the running pieces and fall during the walks,
            and in the first weeks it may go higher than any zone chart suggests for "easy". That is
            normal for an untrained runner and not, by itself, a sign of a problem. Use the talk
            test rather than a heart-rate number at this stage. Once you are running continuously,
            our guides to
            <a href="/treadmill-heart-rate-zones/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill heart rate zones</a>
            and
            <a href="/zone-2-training-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">zone 2 training on a treadmill</a>
            explain how to use heart rate to keep easy days easy — and why, for many beginners,
            true zone 2 is a brisk walk or incline walk for a while yet.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Three sessions, not five</h3>
          <p>
            Run three times a week, with a rest day or a walking day between runs. Muscles and
            lungs recover from a session within a day or two; bone and tendon remodel more slowly,
            and running on consecutive days in the first months gives them no gap to adapt. If you
            want to be on the treadmill more often, walk on the days in between.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="speed-incline" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. What Speed and Incline Should a Beginner Use?</h2>
<p>
            The right numbers come from the talk test, not a chart, but it helps to know where most
            beginners land.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Part of the session</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Typical beginner range</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Pace equivalent</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">How it should feel</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Warm-up walk</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">2.5 to 3.5 mph</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">24:00 to 17:09 per mile</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Easy, building to brisk</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Walking breaks</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3.0 to 3.5 mph</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">20:00 to 17:09 per mile</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Brisk but recovering; breathing settles</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Easy jogging</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">4.0 to 5.5 mph</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">15:00 to 10:55 per mile</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Can speak in short sentences</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Incline</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0 to 1%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">—</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Unchanged for the whole session</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Shorter, older and heavier beginners often jog comfortably nearer 4 mph; taller or
            fitter ones nearer 5.5. Neither is better. The common error is the opposite of what
            people fear: not going too slowly, but choosing a speed that looks respectable and then
            fading halfway through the third interval. A slow jog that you can repeat six times
            does more for you than a fast one you can manage twice. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-pace-and-speed-chart/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill pace and speed chart</a>
            converts any speed to minutes per mile and kilometres if you want to compare with a plan
            or an app.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two more points on speed. First, home treadmill displays are not precisely calibrated,
            so 5 mph on one machine may not be 5 mph on another; judge by feel and use the number
            only to repeat what worked on your own machine. Second, change speed in small steps —
            0.1 or 0.2 mph at a time — rather than jumping, so your stride has time to adjust.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The 1% incline convention, and why it barely matters yet</h3>
          <p>
            You will often read that you should set a treadmill to 1% to make it equivalent to
            running outside. The idea comes from a 1996 study which found that, at faster running
            speeds, a 1% grade made the energy cost of treadmill running match outdoor running.
            The reason is air resistance: outdoors you push through still air, and on a treadmill
            you do not. That effect grows with speed, so it is meaningful for a runner at 7 mph and
            above and small at a beginner's 4 to 5.5 mph.
          </p>
          <p>
            So 1% is a harmless habit and a reasonable default, and some people find a slight
            incline makes the belt feel less as if it is carrying them. It is not a correction you
            need, and 0% is equally fine. What you should not do as a beginner is raise the incline
            to make a slow jog feel like "real" exercise: running uphill loads the calves and
            Achilles tendons more than flat running does, and those are among the tissues most
            likely to complain in the first weeks. Keep incline for walking sessions until you are
            running continuously. Our comparison of
            <a href="/treadmill-vs-running-outside/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill running and running outside</a>
            covers the other differences — wind, terrain and the moving belt — that a grade setting
            does not address.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="on-and-off" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Getting On, Getting Off and the Safety Key</h2>
<p>
            You probably already have a routine for walking. Running adds speed, and speed is what
            turns a small slip into a fall, so it is worth tightening that routine up. The
            controls and basics are covered step by step in our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-use-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to use a treadmill</a>;
            the running-specific points are these.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Clip the safety key to your clothing every time</strong>, at the waist, before
            the belt moves. If you drift too far back or stumble, the cord pulls the key and the
            motor stops. A key left dangling from the console does nothing. Check the cord is long
            enough that it does not pull out during a normal stride, and short enough that it would
            pull out before you reach the back of the deck.</li>
            <li><strong>Start the belt while straddling it</strong>, feet on the side rails, at the
            lowest speed. Step on while holding the rails, walk until you have your rhythm, then let
            go.</li>
            <li><strong>Go from walk to jog gradually.</strong> Raise the speed over ten to twenty
            seconds rather than pressing a preset that jumps straight to your running speed. If your
            machine has quick-speed buttons, use them for walking speeds until you are used to how
            fast they change the belt.</li>
            <li><strong>Come down the same way.</strong> At the end of a running piece, slow to a
            walk before you do anything else — reach for a water bottle, a towel or your phone only
            at walking speed.</li>
            <li><strong>Stop the belt before stepping off</strong>, and wait until it has stopped.
            Stepping off a moving belt, even a slow one, is one of the most common ways people get
            hurt.</li>
            <li><strong>If you lose your footing,</strong> grab the handrails, step your feet onto the
            side rails and let the safety key or the stop button do its job. Do not try to keep up
            with a belt you have fallen behind.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Leave clear space behind the treadmill — manufacturers commonly specify around 6 feet —
            and keep children and pets out of the room while it is running. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-safety-tips/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill safety tips</a>
            cover clearance, keys, children and pets in full.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="form" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. How Should You Run on a Moving Belt?</h2>
<p>
            Running form on a treadmill is mostly the same as outdoors, but a few habits matter more
            on a belt, because the surface is moving towards you and the space is limited. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-running-form/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill running form guide</a>
            goes into each in depth; for a beginner, these are the cues worth holding on to.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Short, quick steps.</strong> The classic beginner fault is overstriding:
            reaching forward and landing on a straight leg, heel well ahead of the body. On a belt
            that also makes it easier to catch the front of the motor hood. Aim to land with your
            foot roughly under your hips. If you want to change your step rate, change it a little:
            studies of step-rate retraining have generally used increases on the order of 5 to 10%,
            not dramatic changes.</li>
            <li><strong>Listen to your feet.</strong> A loud slap on every landing usually means a
            long stride or a heavy heel strike. Quieter landings are a simple, useful target.</li>
            <li><strong>Stay in the middle of the deck.</strong> Close enough to the front that you
            are not near the rear roller, far enough back that your toes do not touch the hood.
            Drifting back is usually a sign you are tiring — slow the belt rather than fight it.</li>
            <li><strong>Look ahead.</strong> Pick a point at eye level in front of you. Looking down
            at your feet or the console tips your head and shifts your balance.</li>
            <li><strong>Hands off the rails.</strong> Holding the rails while running alters your
            posture and stride, and if you need them to keep up, the belt is too fast. They are for
            getting on and off and for emergencies.</li>
            <li><strong>Relaxed arms and shoulders.</strong> Elbows bent at about ninety degrees,
            swinging forwards and backwards rather than across the body, shoulders down.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            One treadmill-specific trap is worth knowing about: the belt does not slow when you tire.
            Outdoors, you decelerate naturally at the end of a hard interval; on a treadmill the
            speed holds until you press something, and fatigue shows up as a shortening, scuffing,
            drifting stride instead. When form starts to go, take the speed down by a few tenths or
            start the walking break early. That is good training judgment, not failure.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="progression" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. How to Progress Over 4 to 8 Weeks</h2>
<p>
            Progress by adding running time, not speed, and by changing one thing at a time. The
            ladder below is one simple way to do that. Each rung is a session you repeat; you move up
            a rung when you have completed the current one twice, finished it able to talk, and woken
            the next day with nothing worse than general tiredness. At three sessions a week, many
            people climb about a rung a week, which puts the top of the ladder around week eight.
            Others repeat rungs and take longer, and that is the ladder working, not failing.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Rung</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Between a 5-minute warm-up and cool-down walk</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Running in total</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">6 × (1 min jog, 2 min walk)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">6 min</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">2</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">6 × (90 sec jog, 2 min walk)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">9 min</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">6 × (2 min jog, 2 min walk)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">12 min</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">4</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5 × (3 min jog, 2 min walk)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">15 min</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">4 × (4 min jog, 90 sec walk)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">16 min</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">6</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">4 × (5 min jog, 90 sec walk)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">20 min</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">7</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3 × (8 min jog, 2 min walk)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">24 min</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">8</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">2 × (12 min jog, 2 min walk), then 20 minutes continuous when ready</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">20 to 24 min</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Keep the jogging speed the same all the way up the ladder. Most people find that their
            "easy" speed creeps up on its own over the weeks — the same effort, a slightly higher
            number — and that is the right way for speed to arrive. Deliberately pushing the pace
            and adding time in the same week is how beginners overreach.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The progression rules</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Time before pace.</strong> Build to 20 or 30 minutes of continuous easy
            running before you think about running faster.</li>
            <li><strong>One change at a time.</strong> Longer intervals, or a faster speed, or an
            incline — never two in the same week.</li>
            <li><strong>Small steps.</strong> A common rule of thumb is to increase weekly running
            time by no more than about 10%. It is a rough guide rather than a law, and early on the
            ladder's jumps are bigger than that in percentage terms but small in minutes.</li>
            <li><strong>Repeat freely.</strong> A rung repeated because it was hard is time well
            spent. Dropping back a rung after a bad week or a few days off is normal.</li>
            <li><strong>Three runs a week.</strong> Hold at three running days until you have been
            running continuously for a couple of months.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If you would rather follow a fixed calendar than a ladder, the well-known nine-week
            walk-to-run plan does the same job with set sessions for each week; our
            <a href="/couch-to-5k-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">couch to 5K on a treadmill</a>
            guide lays out all nine weeks with treadmill speeds. And if even the first rung is too
            much, start a step earlier with our
            <a href="/treadmill-workout-for-beginners/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">beginner treadmill workouts</a>,
            which spend the first weeks building a walking base before introducing short jogs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="too-much" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. What Are the Signs You Are Doing Too Much?</h2>
<p>
            Some soreness is expected in the first weeks of running: a general ache in the calves,
            thighs or glutes a day or two after a session that eases as you warm up and is gone in a
            few days. That is muscles adapting to a new load. The signs below are different, and
            each is a reason to reduce or rest rather than push on.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Pain that gets worse as you run</strong>, rather than easing after the
            warm-up.</li>
            <li><strong>A tender spot on a bone</strong>, especially along the shin or on the top of
            the foot, that hurts to press. Diffuse shin ache is common in new runners; a precise,
            pinpoint spot of bone tenderness needs a professional opinion, because it can be an early
            stress injury.</li>
            <li><strong>Heel or arch pain on your first steps in the morning</strong>, or Achilles
            pain and stiffness that lasts beyond the first few minutes of walking.</li>
            <li><strong>Knee pain</strong> at the front or outside of the knee that appears at the
            same point in every run.</li>
            <li><strong>Limping</strong> during a run, or changing how you run to avoid pain.</li>
            <li><strong>Fatigue that accumulates</strong> rather than clears: heavy legs at the start
            of every session, poor sleep, irritability, or a resting heart rate noticeably higher
            than usual for several days.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            For a niggle, the usual response is to drop back a rung or two, add a rest day, and
            replace a run with a walk for a few days. If the pain does not settle within a week or
            so, keeps returning at the same point, or is sharp, swollen or bony, see a doctor or a
            physical therapist rather than running through it. A week off early costs nothing; a
            stress injury can cost two months.
          </p>
          <p>
            It is also worth checking session length. A beginner's running sessions do not need to
            be long — the ladder tops out at around half an hour including walking — and our guide to
            <a href="/how-long-should-i-run-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how long you should run on a treadmill</a>
            sets out sensible durations by goal and experience for when you are ready to go
            further.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="shoes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. What Shoes Should You Wear to Start Running?</h2>
<p>
            Running shoes that fit you. That is most of the answer. You do not need the most
            cushioned, the most expensive or a "treadmill-specific" model, but you do need a shoe
            designed for running rather than a court shoe, a fashion sneaker or a worn-out pair you
            walk the dog in.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Fit first.</strong> A thumb's width of room in front of the longest toe, a
            heel that does not slip, and no pressure points. Feet swell during exercise, so try shoes
            on later in the day, with the socks you will run in.</li>
            <li><strong>Comfort over features.</strong> Researchers who study running shoes have
            proposed comfort as a sensible way for most runners to choose. If a shoe feels wrong in the shop, it
            is unlikely to feel right at minute twenty.</li>
            <li><strong>Breathability.</strong> Indoors there is no breeze, and feet get hot. A
            lighter, more ventilated upper is often more comfortable on a treadmill than a
            weatherproof road shoe.</li>
            <li><strong>No big changes at once.</strong> If you are used to a high-drop, cushioned
            walking shoe, a flat minimal shoe is a large change for your calves and Achilles tendons
            on top of the new load of running. Introduce one change at a time.</li>
            <li><strong>Replace when worn.</strong> Commonly cited guidance is somewhere around 300
            to 500 miles, but the midsole's feel matters more than the number. A beginner running
            three times a week will not reach that for many months.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Our roundup of the
            <a href="/best-running-shoes-for-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best running shoes for treadmill running</a>
            explains how belt cushioning and shoe cushioning interact and lists current models
            across the range, and our guide to
            <a href="/how-often-to-replace-running-shoes/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how often to replace running shoes</a>
            covers the signs a pair is done.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-next" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. What Comes After the First Two Months?</h2>
<p>
            Once you can run 20 to 30 minutes continuously at an easy pace, three times a week, with
            nothing hurting, you have a running habit. What comes next depends on what you want from
            it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>More time, not more speed, for a while.</strong> Extend one run a week by five
            minutes at a time and keep the other two as they are. Most of your running should stay
            at a conversational pace for months; the faster sessions — intervals, tempo runs and
            hills — are worth adding only once easy running feels routine, and then one a week.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Introduce incline carefully.</strong> A short block at 2 or 3% within an easy
            run is a gentle first step towards hill work. Treat it as the week's one change.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Try running outside, if you want to.</strong> The treadmill is an excellent place
            to learn, but the road adds wind, turns and uneven ground, and the same speed usually
            feels a little harder outdoors. Start outdoor runs a little slower than your treadmill
            pace and keep them on similar effort.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Keep the treadmill's advantages.</strong> A controlled pace, a predictable surface
            and no weather mean the treadmill remains the easiest place to do exact intervals or keep
            running through winter, and its cushioned deck is kinder than concrete for many beginners.
            Whatever you do next, the habits that got you here — slow easy running, three days a
            week, one change at a time, and paying attention to warning signs — still apply.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How fast should a beginner run on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Slow enough to talk in short sentences. For many beginners that is somewhere around 4 to 5.5 mph, roughly a 15- to 11-minute mile, with walking breaks at about 3 to 3.5 mph. Those are typical ranges rather than targets: shorter, older or heavier beginners are often nearer the bottom, and the talk test matters more than the number.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does it take to be able to run 30 minutes on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a beginner who already walks regularly, building from short walk-run intervals to about 20 minutes of continuous easy running commonly takes four to eight weeks at three sessions a week, and 30 minutes a few weeks more, often eight to ten weeks in total. Repeating weeks is normal. Adding running time slowly, rather than speed, is what gets most people there without injury.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I set the treadmill to 1% incline when I start running?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is optional. The 1% convention comes from research showing it matches the energy cost of outdoor running at faster speeds, where air resistance matters. At beginner speeds the difference is small, so 0% or 1% are both fine. Avoid raising the incline further while you are learning to run, because it adds load to the calves and Achilles tendons.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many days a week should a beginner run on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Three running days a week on non-consecutive days is a common and sensible starting point. Bones and tendons adapt more slowly than heart and lungs, and the rest day between runs gives them time. If you want to use the treadmill more often, walk on the days in between rather than adding extra runs.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it OK to hold the handrails while running on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only to get on and off or to steady yourself in an emergency. Holding the rails while running changes your posture and stride and usually means the belt is too fast for you. Slow the speed until you can run comfortably hands-free, and keep the safety key clipped to your clothing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">When should I see a doctor before starting to run?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Talk to a doctor first if you have a heart, lung, kidney or metabolic condition, high blood pressure, take medication that affects heart rate, are pregnant, have a joint problem or recent injury, or have been inactive for a long time. Stop and get checked if you feel chest pain, unusual breathlessness, dizziness or an irregular heartbeat while exercising.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes When Starting to Run on a Treadmill</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most new runners who stop do so for one of a handful of reasons, and almost all of them come from doing too much too soon rather than too little.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running the intervals too fast</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Choosing a speed that looks respectable, then fading by the third interval, is the most common beginner error. The belt holds the speed even as you tire, so form breaks down instead. Pick a pace where you can talk in short sentences, even if it feels absurdly slow, and let speed come on its own over the weeks.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running on consecutive days</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Heart and lungs recover quickly, so a beginner often feels ready to run again the next day before their shins, Achilles tendons and feet are. Run three times a week with a rest or walking day between runs for the first couple of months, and walk on the in-between days if you want to stay on the machine.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Adding speed and time in the same week</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Longer intervals and a faster belt, or a new incline on top of a longer run, stack two new loads at once. Change one thing at a time, build running time before pace, and repeat a session or a week whenever it felt hard. A repeated week costs nothing; an overuse injury can cost two months.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating the safety key as optional</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A safety key left on the console does nothing if you stumble at running speed. Clip it to your clothing before the belt moves, every session, start the belt while straddling it, and slow to a walk before reaching for anything. Stop the belt completely before stepping off, and keep children and pets out of the room.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

